Estuaria is known for its low elevation and flat topography, with only about 50% of its land exceeding 1 m (3.3 ft) above sea level, and nearly 26% falling below sea level. Most of the areas below sea level, known as [[W:Polder|guanyadas]], are the result of [[W:land reclamation|land reclamation]] that began in the 18th century. Colloquially or informally, Estuaria is occasionally referred to by the ''{{W|pars pro toto}}'' [[Nova Marmoca]].

[[Politics of Estuaria|Estuarian political culture]] mostly differs on a [[Estuarian nationalism|nationalist]]-vs-confederalist continuum, rather than a left-vs-right continuum. Debates regarding Estuarian identity have played a large role in politics. Estuarian society's cohesion and specificity is based on three of its unique statutory documents: the [[Estuarian Charter of Human Rights and Freedoms]], the [[Charter of the Creole Language]], and the [[Humanitarian Decleration of Estuaria|Humanitarian Declaration (1863)]]. Until the [[PREC|early 1920s]], [[Catharism]] played a large role in the social and cultural institutions in Estuaria. The country has a tradition of {{W|pillarisation}} and a long record of social tolerance, having legalised {{W|abortion}}, {{W|prostitution}} and {{W|human euthanasia}}, along with maintaining a liberal drug policy. Estuaria has never permitted {{W|capital punishment}}, Estuarian regions rank among the highest in national indices of {{W|press freedom}}, economic freedom, {{W|human development}} and {{W|quality of life}}. 

Estuaria's official language is {{W|Catalan language|Volisanian}}; [[Estuarian Volisanian]] is the regional variety, while there are several {{W|creole languages}}, most notably [[Estuarian Creole]]. The economy of Estuaria is mainly supported by its large service sector and varied industrial sector. For exports, it leans on these key industries: aeronautics, hydroelectricity, oil, pharmaceuticals, aluminum and paper. Estuaria is well known for producing {{W|roses}}, {{W|sugar cane}} and {{W|sunflowers}}, for its comedy, and for making {{W|rugby}} one of the most popular sports in Balisca. It is also renowned for its culture; producing significant works in literature, music, films, TV shows, festivals, folklore, and more.
==History==

=== Pre-history and Indigenous peoples ===

=== Exploration and colonization of Estuaria ===
{{Main|Colonial Balisca|Catharism in Estuaria|}}
The first Volisanian expedition to reach the Marañón delta arrived in 1528, when the explorer [[Narcís de Vilallonga]] located the mouth of the [[Marañón River]] after a disastrous overland march from the northern coast of Bayara. Vilallonga's party, reduced by disease, drowning, and conflict with the various [[Kurupiranga]] communities along the river, numbered fewer than forty when they reached the sea. The expedition's surviving pilot, [[Bernat de Perpinyà]], produced the first crude chart of the Marañón's mouth and the labyrinthine network of channels, bayous, and lagoons that would later be called the ''[[Aiguamolls]]''—the wetlands.

In 1542, the conquistador [[Hug de Montcada]] led a larger expedition into the [[Marañón basin]], following the river northward from the Bayaran coast through [[Kurupiranga]] and [[Tapajóara]] territories, then descending the Marañón to its delta in 1543. Montcada's chronicles described the wetlands in terms that would shape Volisanian perceptions for a century and a half: "''un país d'aigua i fang, on la terra no és terra i l'aigua no és aigua''"—a country of water and mud, where the land is not land and the water is not water. Volisanian interest in the Estuarian littoral faded for nearly a century and a half after Montcada's return. The wetlands offered no gold, no silver, no easily exploitable labor force; the indigenous peoples of the Aiguamolls—skilled canoe navigators who knew every channel and current—could not be cornered or enslaved in the way that highland and plantation peoples could. Colonial attention remained fixed on the richer prizes of Bayara and the [[Levante, Balisca|east coast]].

==== The Great Disappointment and the charter system ====
{{See also|Great Disappointment|Volisanian colonial charters|Fosian contact with Abyala}}
Volisanian neglect of the Estuarian wetlands must be understood in the context of the broader colonial competition that shaped the Mesogean presence in Abyala. By the time of Montcada's expedition, the [[Great Disappointment]]—the Volisanian Crown's realization that Fosian powers (led by [[Zong]]) were already well established in the New World, with trading networks, settlements, and political alliances that predated Volisanian contact by decades—had transformed the Crown's approach to colonization. The slow, extractive model of conquest that had characterized the early Bayaran expeditions was insufficient; the Fosians were faster, more adaptable, and already embedded in the indigenous economies of the continent.

The Volisanian response was the [[Volisanian charter system|charter system]]—a program of grants, concessions, and licenses issued to other Mesogean powers, merchant companies, and religious orders, authorizing them to explore, settle, and administer territories in Abyala on the Crown's behalf. The charter system served multiple purposes: it expanded the Volisanian colonial footprint without requiring direct expenditure from the Crown; it recruited the entrepreneurial energy of the other Mesogean peoples—[[Jacan|Jacanos]], [[Nabia|Nabians]], [[Andalus|Andalusis]], [[Monsora|Monsorans]]—who might otherwise have established competing colonial claims; and it created a web of dependent colonial entities that owed allegiance (and revenue) to the Volisanian Crown while bearing the costs and risks of settlement themselves.

The charter system produced a patchwork of colonial enterprises across Abyala: Andalusian trading companies in the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]], Xacano sugar plantations in the [[Marañón basin]], an a variety of missionary orders concentrated on both coasts. The quality of governance, the treatment of indigenous peoples, and the economic productivity of these enterprises varied enormously—from the relatively benign Catharist and Muslim reductions to the brutal Xacano plantation system. What they shared was the legal fiction that they operated under Volisanian sovereignty, a fiction that the Crown enforced when convenient and ignored when not.

The Estuarian wetlands, deemed worthless by the major charter companies, were granted in a series of small concessions to minor Mesogean entities—religious orders, failed merchant companies, and, after the [[Reapers' War]], the Cathar communities expelled from Bayara. The wetlands' marginal status in the Volisanian colonial system would prove to be the foundation of their distinctive society.

==== Cathar settlement ====
{{See also|Reapers' War|Tarraco exodus|Cerdanya#Volisanian colonization}}[[File:Ruínas_1.jpg|thumb|The ruins of the Catharist reduction at [[Sant Domènec de les Missions]], [[Mesopotàmia]], destroyed during the [[Reapers' War]]]]The Volisanian neglect of the Estuarian wetlands ended not through renewed colonial ambition but through the [[Reapers' War]] (1578–1608)—the protracted conflict between Cathar and Catholic colonists in the Marañón basin that culminated in the [[Peace of Santa Creu]] and the expulsion of the Cathar population from Bayara. The treaty's terms, which granted the defeated Cathars the right to settle in "such territories as are not presently claimed by Catholic colonists," effectively consigned them to the Aiguamolls—the vast, malarial, flood-prone wetlands that the Catholic planters of Bayara had deemed worthless. The Volisanian Crown, eager to populate the empty littoral and create a buffer between the Bayaran plantations and the Fosian-influenced interior, ratified the terms and issued the Cathars a colonial charter—the [[Charter of the Wetlands|''Carta de l'Aiguamoll'']] (Charter of the Wetlands)—granting them communal land rights and limited self-governance in exchange for nominal allegiance and a modest annual tribute.

The Cathar migration into Estuaria—known in Cathar historiography as the Marañón exodus ({{lang|ca|Èxode del Maranyó}}), after the Bayaran city from which the largest convoy departed—began in 1609 and continued in waves through the 1620s. The refugees carried with them the two resources that would prove decisive: a theological commitment to communal landholding (the Cathar tradition of ''[[benestança]]'', in which property was held by the community of believers rather than by individuals) and a body of hydraulic knowledge derived from the Cathar communities of the [[Mozbayani]] coast, who had centuries of experience with drainage, levee-building, and land reclamation in the low-lying coastal plains of eastern Illypnia.

The first permanent Cathar settlement in Estuaria was [[Xàbia]], established in 1611 on a natural levee of the lower Marañón—the same year that the [[Captaincy General of Cerdanya]] abolished slavery within its jurisdiction. Xàbia served as the gateway for subsequent settlement: a fortified river port with a communal granary, a levee system protecting the settlement from seasonal flooding, and a ''[[casa de consolament]]''—the Cathar meeting house that served as church, school, and community center. The site was chosen for its elevation—a relative term in Estuaria, where the highest ground might stand only two meters above the flood line—and for its position at the junction of the Marañón and the [[Riu Negre]], the "black river" whose tannin-stained waters carried nutrients from the interior swamps.

The Cathar settlement pattern differed radically from the plantation model that dominated Bayara. Where Catholic colonists established great estates worked by enslaved Kaftians, the Cathars built compact, communal villages—''[[pobles de benestança]]''—clustered on natural levees and connected by a network of drainage canals, levees, and raised pathways. The wetlands were not conquered but accommodated: the Cathars learned to read the flood cycles, to build on pilings, to construct their homes on raised earthen platforms (''[[terres altes]]'') that kept them above the waterline during the annual inundation. The result was a landscape that looked nothing like the ordered grid of a colonial plantation and everything like an extension of the wetland ecosystem—human habitation woven into the water rather than imposed upon it.

The early decades were brutal. [[W:Malaria|Malaria]], [[W:Yellow fever|yellow fever]], and periodic flooding killed an estimated thirty percent of the first wave of settlers. The Cathars' communal structure, however, proved resilient: the survivors absorbed the land and obligations of the dead, and the ''benestança'' system ensured that no family was left without support. The [[W:Cathar Perfect|Cathar ''perfecti'']]—trained in herbal medicine and the care of the sick—played a critical role in the survival of the early settlements, developing treatments for malaria from the bark of the ''[[W:Cinchona|quina]]'' tree (a practice likely learned from indigenous healers) and establishing quarantine protocols that limited the spread of epidemic disease.

==== Expansion and the ''guanyada'' system ====
[[File:Estuarian Atlas.jpg|left|thumb|The [[Estuarian Atlas]], depicting the [[Estuaria delta|Aín–Canyada–Daimús delta]] in the 1640s]]
By the 1640s, the Cathar population of Estuaria had stabilized and begun to expand. The second and third waves of settlement—composed of Cathars expelled from other Bayaran cities, Mozbayani-speaking refugees from the [[Andalusi–Mauretanian wars]], and a small but significant number of indentured Illypnians and enslaved Kaftians who had fled servitude in the Bayaran plantations—pushed deeper into the wetlands, establishing new ''pobles de benestança'' along the Marañón's tributaries and in the vast [[W:Freshwater marsh|freshwater marshes]] of the interior.

The key innovation of this period was the development of the [[W:Polder|''guanyada'' system]]—a technique of land reclamation borrowed from the Mozbayani hydraulic tradition and adapted to the Estuarian wetlands. The Cathars constructed encircling [[W:Levee|levees]] around sections of marshland, then used wind-powered pumps (and later, animal-powered scoop wheels) to drain the water from the enclosed area, exposing the fertile alluvial soil beneath. The reclaimed land—called a ''guanyada'' ({{Literal translation|gained land}})—was held collectively by the ''benestança'' and allocated to member families for cultivation. The polder system transformed the Estuarian economy: by 1700, the drained wetlands produced [[W:Rice|rice]], [[W:Sugarcane|sugarcane]], [[W:Indigo|indigo]], and [[W:Cotton|cotton]] in quantities that rivaled the output of the Bayaran plantations—achieved without enslaved labor and through communal organization rather than private capital.

The guanyada system also transformed the Estuarian landscape. The wetlands, which had been a uniform expanse of marsh and open water, became a patchwork of drained fields, drainage canals, and retained wetland zones—the latter preserved as fish ponds, waterfowl habitat, and sources of thatch and reeds for construction. The resulting landscape, visible from the air today, is one of the most distinctive in Balisca: a geometric pattern of rectangular guanyada, each surrounded by its levee and canal, interspersed with the natural wetlands that the Cathars chose not to drain—areas too deep, too unstable, or too ecologically valuable to reclaim.

==== Relations with indigenous peoples ====
{{See also|Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Jasyruwai people|Tapajóara culture}}
The Cathar settlement of Estuaria unfolded in a complex relationship with the indigenous peoples of the wetlands—primarily belonging with the [[Kurupiranga]] [[W:Panethnicity|panethnicity]] of the riverine interior and the [[Tapajóara culture|Tapajóara]] of the raised-field systems along the Marañón's middle reaches. Unlike the Catholic colonists of Bayara, who sought to enslave or displace indigenous populations, the Cathars arrived as refugees and were in no position to impose their will by force. The result was a pattern of accommodation, exchange, and occasional conflict that differed markedly from the plantation frontier.
[[File:Sistema mixt-Jasyruwai.png|thumb|''Sistema Mixt''-''Poble'' ''Jasyruwai'' (1869), depicting [[Xasiruguà]] village]]
The indigenous [[Jasyruwai people]], who had observed the Volisanian expeditions of Vilallonga and Montcada from their canoes, initially regarded the Cathar settlers with cautious curiosity. The Cathars' communal landholding practices, their [[Catharism#Opposition to slavery|refusal to own slaves]], and their willingness to trade manufactured goods for food and local knowledge created conditions for cooperation that did not exist within the other Mesogean colonies. The Jasyruwai taught the Cathars the routes through the ''Aiguamolls'', the locations of high ground, the habits of the river, and the properties of the quina tree—knowledge that was essential to the survival of the early settlements.

The relationship was not without tension. The Cathars' guanyada system, by draining wetlands that had been fishing and hunting grounds for the indigenous peoples, reduced the resources available to indigenous communities. The  response was typically to relocate—moving their villages to undrained wetlands further from the Cathar settlements—rather than to resist directly. This pattern of gradual displacement, achieved without overt violence but through the steady transformation of the landscape, would later be characterized by indigenous scholars as a form of [[W:Ecological imperialism|ecological imperialism]]—the replacement of one landscape and one way of life with another, not through conquest but through drainage and transformation of the environment.

The Tapajóara, whose raised-field agriculture (''[[mbaraka-ité]]'') represented a sophisticated indigenous approach to wetland cultivation, had a more ambivalent relationship with the Cathar settlements. The two groups shared a common interest in managing water and cultivating wetland soils, and there is evidence of technological exchange: the Cathars adopted elements of the raised-field system for use in terrain too wet for conventional drainage, while the Tapajóara incorporated levee and canal techniques from the Cathar guanyada model. The resulting hybrid—known in Estuarian agricultural practice as the ''[[sistema mixt]]''—remains in use in parts of the Marañón basin to this day.

