The [[2025 Baliscan census]] reported an Estuarian population of 12,985,820, ranking as the second-least populous community in Balisca (only ahead of [[Pindorama]]). Estuaria's [[W:population growth|rate of population growth]] has been averaging around one percent growth per year for the last two decades. Since 2015, the majority of population growth in Estuaria has been attributed to immigration; 54% of all children born in 2018 in Estuaria were to immigrant parents. About 70 percent of the community's population lives in urban areas, a significantly lower number than the rest of the Baliscan Confederation.

In the [[2025 Baliscan census]], 88.4% of the residents of Estuaria claimed it's nationality, and 84% declared that they fluently spoke or had a working knowledge of the [[Volisanian language]]. In the same census, 42.21% of the almost 18 million inhabitants of Estuaria claimed to be descendants of another ancestry other than [[Volisanian Baliscans|Volisanian]]. That number includes 1.6 million who declared to be [[Jacano Baliscans|Jacano]] as a national-ethnic identification (489,000 as single ethnicity and 1,200,000 a second ethnicity) and 700,000 [[Beron Baliscans|Beron]] (575,000 as single ethnicity). 
===Ethnography===
{{Main|Ethnography of Balisca}}

===Languages===
{{See also|Estuarian Creole}}
Estuaria differs from other Baliscan communities in that {{W|Catalan language|Volisanian}} is the only official and preponderant language, while [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] has increasingly predominated in the rest of Balisca. Volisanian is the common language, understood and spoken by 94.46% of the population. Estuaria is the only Baliscan community whose population is mainly [[Volisanophone]]; 9,480,425 people (78.1% of the population) recorded it as their sole native language in the 2025 census, and 9,711,063 (80.0%) recorded that they spoke it most often at home. Knowledge of Volisanian is widespread even among those who do not speak it natively; in 2011, about 84.4% of the total population reported being able to speak Volisanian, alone or in combination with other languages. A considerable number of Estuarian residents consider themselves to be bilingual in Volisanian and [[Estuarian Creole|Creole]]: about 42.6% of the population (0,000,000 people) report knowing both languages.

Several other {{W|Romance languages|Mesogean languages}} are spoken throughout Estuaria; the largest of these minority languages are indigenously [[Illypnia|Illypnian]]; [[Mozbayani language|Mozbayani]], [[W:Judeo-Catalan|Judaeo-Volisanian]], and {{W|Aragonese language|J/Xacano}}. Since 1960s, these languages have been protected as endangered within Estuaria, and have since seen a resurgence in the number of native speakers. [[Estuarian Creole]], a {{W|pidgin}}, was additionally recognized as a {{W|national language|community language}} in 1997. Prior to the [[Baliscan Revolution]], Volisanian was the only language used by the Estuarian government in the writing of laws, decrees and announcements. The absence of the widespread practice of [[W:Multilingualism|multilingualism]] that is prevalent in other Baliscan regions can be attributed to the strong historical animosity between Bayaran Jacano-speaking and Volisanian-speaking settlers in Balisca, and the rise of [[W:Nationalist historiography|nationalist historiography]] in Estuaria.

==== Estuarian Creole ====
{{Main|Estuarian Creole|Languages of Balisca}}

The interaction between Cathar settlers, indigenous peoples, and the small but growing population of indentured Illypnians and escaped Kaftians in the Estuarian wetlands produced a distinctive linguistic environment; Estuarian Creole ({{lang|ecr|Créyòl}}) emerged in the late 17th century as a contact language—simpler in structure than Maroniko but sharing its Sabir-derived base, with significant contributions from Mozbayani (the liturgical and administrative language of the Cathar community), Kurupiranga (the language of the wetlands' indigenous inhabitants), and the Bayani of the Kaftian traders who operated along the Marañón. The creole became the lingua franca of the wetlands, used in markets, along the river, and in the polder communities, while Volisanian remained the language of formal governance and Mozbayani the language of Cathar worship.

===Religion===
{{See also|Religion in Balisca}}{{Pie chart|thumb=right|caption=Religion in Estuaria (2022)|label1=[[wikipedia:Irreligion|No religion]]|value1=32.6|color1=DodgerBlue|label2=[[w:Catharism|Catharism]]|value2=28.8|color2=Crimson|label3=[[w:Roman Catholic Church|Catholic]]|value3=24.3|color3=DarkOrchid|label4=[[wikipedia:Islam|Islam]]|value4=10.5|color4=DarkGreen|label5=Other Christian churches (incl. [[wikipedia:Protestantism|Protestantism]], [[w:Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern]] and [[w:Oriental Orthodox Churches|Oriental Orthodox]])|value5=2.9|color5=Turquoise|label6=Believers, not members of other religions|value6=0.1|color6=Chartreuse|label7=[[w:Hinduism|Hinduism]]|value7=0.3|color7=Orange|label8=[[w:Sikhism|Sikhism]]|value8=0.1|color8=DarkKhaki|label9=[[w:Buddhism|Buddhism]]|value9=10.1|color9=Yellow|label10=[[Jews in Balisca|Jewish]]|value10=0.1|color10=Hotpink|label11=Others/undeclared|value11=0.1|color11=Black}}

[[Christianity in Balisca|Christianity]] has been the predominant religion in Estuaria for more than four centuries, though it is not nearly as actively practiced today as it was. The Christian population of Estuaria is predominately [[Catharism|Cathars]] (86%), with the next largest group being [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholics]] (11%), with a smaller number of adherents of [[W:Eastern Orthodoxy|Eastern Orthodoxy]] and [[W:Protestantism|Protestantism]]. In the 1980s, more than 90% of Estuaria's still declared themselves to be Christian, in the 1990s this proportion had fallen to less than 80%, falling by the 2000s to around 70%, and in the 2010s, to less than 50%. According to a survey held in 2022 by [[Confederal Institute of Statistics|ICS]] and [[L'Institut estuaria d'opinió pública]] (Ieop), 46% of the total population of Estuaria was Christian as of that year; at the same time 32.6% of the population had no religion (atheism or agnosticism), 10.6% were Muslims, 10.1% were [[Baiduism in Balisca|Baiduist]], 2.5% were followers of other faiths, and the remaining 0.4% were undecided about their faith.

With the gradual liberalization of Estuaria since the 1880s, the proportion of non-Christians has steadily increased. In 2017, in a ICS poll, 32% of respondents answered "I believe there is a God", 22% answered "I believe there is some sort of spirit or life force", and 46% answered "I don't believe there is any sort of spirit, God or life force".
== Culture and society ==
{{Main|Culture of Balisca}}

{{See also|Society of Balisca|Baliscan values}}
[[File:Correfoc_2023.jpg|left|thumb|The tradition of [[W:Correfoc|correfocs]], "fire runs" developed in Estuaria amongst Cathar colonists. 

In the ''correfoc'', a group of individuals will dress as [[W:Devil|devils]] and light up [[W:Fireworks|fireworks]] – fixed on devil's [[W:pitchforks|pitchforks]] or strung above the route. Dancing to the sound of a rhythmic drum group, they set off their fireworks among crowds of spectators. The spectators attempt to get as close as possible to the devils, running with the fire. ]]
Estuaria is densely populated and just under one-fourth of its territory lies below sea level and has to be defended from nature. This has led to a rational and collective approach to [[W:Spatial planning|spatial planning]], and particularly to [[W:Flood control|water management]].

In [[W:Urban planning|urban planning]], this is seen in:

* the retention of the [[W:Social ownership|social ownership]] of most land, which facilitates comprehensive development and socializes increases in land prices;
* the widespread accessibility of housing through [[W:Housing cooperative|housing cooperatives]] (co-ops)
* rational mobility, including a dense [[Balisca#Transportation|railway]] network, reliable rural bus connection, and, since the 1970s, some world's best [[W:Cycling infrastructure|cycling infrastructure]]. Almost everyone cycles as a matter of course, and cycling has a [[W:Modal share|modal share]] of 27% of all trips (urban and rural) nationwide.

[[File:Ilop_rice.jpg|thumb|260x260px|[[Waji rice]], a traditionally [[Navassa|Navassan]] dish combining {{W|paella|Bayaran paella}} and [[West Kaftian rice]], is considered the national dish of the Balisca]]

Estuarian culture minimizes ostentatious displays of status and wealth difference, and have a low [[W:Power distance|power distance]]. There is a widespread consensus and tolerance of difference and [[W:right to privacy|respect for privacy]].

As the community's watery [[Estuaria#Environment and geography|geography]] dictates, Estuaria has a strong [[W:Nautical culture|nautical culture]]; it heavily influences folklore, traditions, and culinary styles.

The Cathar colony struggled for decades to survive. Conditions were harsh, the climate and soil were unsuitable for certain crops the colonists knew, and they suffered from regional [[W:Tropical diseases|tropical diseases]]. Both colonists and the self-liberated Maroons had high mortality rates. Their was a [[Maroon Wars|continuous stream]] of liberated Kaftian which resulted in a high proportion of native Kaftians born in Central Kaftia, that continued to practice their culture in new surroundings. Creole culture emerged in Estauria as an amalgamation of [[Kafto-Baliscans|Kaftian]], [[Volisanian Baliscans|Volisanian]], [[Beron Baliscans|Beron]], other smaller Illypnian populations, [[Mozbayán|Mozbayáns]] and Indigenous cultures. 

==Environment and geography==
{{Main|Geography of Balisca}}

[[File:Bonrepòs.png|left|thumb|280x280px|[[Bonrepòs]], a commune in [[Canyars]]. It sits on a [[W:Polder|polder]] below sea level and is protected by a {{convert|3|m|abbr=on}} tall {{W|levee|sea wall}}.]]
Estuaria is geographically very low relative to {{W|sea level}} and comprises the flattest territory within Balisca, with about 26% of its area and 31% of its population located below sea level, and only about 50% of its land exceeding one metre above sea level. The mainland part of the country is for the most part flat, with the exception of foothills in the far southeast, up to a height of no more than {{convert|121|m|abbr=on}}, and some low hill ranges in the central parts. Most of the areas below sea level are man-made, caused by {{W|peat}} extraction or achieved through {{W|land reclamation}}. Since the late 18th century, large {{W|polder}} areas are preserved through elaborate drainage systems that include {{W|dikes}}, {{W|canal}}s and pumping stations. Nearly 10% of the country's land area is reclaimed from the sea and from lakes, the largest of which is the [[Mar Menor Works]]. 
[[File:Mar Menor works seawall.jpg|thumb|A segment of the [[Mar Menor Works]], which protects the [[Estuarian Wetlands]] from [[W:Erosion|erosion]] and [[W:Land loss|wetlands loss]]. ]]
The emergent landmass of Mesopotàmia was Orange Island, a low-relief island sitting atop the carbonate Estuarian Platform which emerged about 34 to 28 million years ago. It became the first landmass of Estuaria to emerge from the sea, leading to vast lakes in it's interior and extensive areas of coastal {{W|marshlands}}. These contain a rich Baliscan {{W|biome|biota}}; typical examples include birds such as {{W|storks}}, {{W|toucans}} and {{W|egrets}}. There are also many species of {{W|tree frogs}}, and fish such as {{W|Salminus brasiliensis|golden dorado}} and {{W|salmon}}. In more elevated areas, fire is a natural process in the landscape and has produced extensive areas of longleaf pine forest and wet savannas. These support an exceptionally large number of plant species, including many species of {{W|Orchidaceae|terrestrial orchids}} and {{W|carnivorous plants}}.

Much of the country was originally formed by the estuaries of three large Baliscan rivers: the [[Aín (river)|Aín]] (''Ahín''), the [[Daimús (river)|Daimús]] (''Daimuz'') and the [[Canyada (river)|Canyada]] (''Cañada''), as well as their tributaries. The western areas of Estuaria are to this day a {{W|river delta}} of these three rivers, the [[Estuaria delta|Aín–Canyada–Daimús delta]].

Estuaria's coast, called the ''Costa Criolla'' ("{{W|Creole peoples|Creole}} Coast") is made up of a system of marshes and swamps that are collectively referred to as the [[Estuarian Marshes]]. Further inland, Estuaria's interior is dominated by low-lying plains that are dotted with {{W|marshes}} and {{W|bogs}}. The largest river in Estuaria, the Aín, rises from the [[Serra Nevada]] and flows westward, before splitting and  emptying into the Mar de Colores, graully forming the Estuarian delta. Over the last 4,500 years, the course of the river has {{W|Avulsion (river)|avulsed}} its course in search of shorter route to the sea approximately every 500–1000 years, which gradually allowed the formation of the entire Crisol Delta. However, with the advent of European colonization, a complex system of embankments (called diques) and drainage canals, called the ''sistema de desagüe'' ("drainage system") was built to contain the flow of the river and allow for farming has deprived the delta of the sediments needed to combat erosion, which in turn contributes to the shrinking of the Crisol coast.

Estuaria has been increasingly threatened with coast erosion and habitat loss: In June 2018, the fishing town of Ripollès was declared uninhabitable by the government of Estuaria, and its population had to be relocated further inland. Several other settlements within Estuaria face similar threats, such as the [[Old City of Navassa|Old City]] of [[Navassa]], which is built on several reclaimed islands, and Estuaria's largest city [[Aigües]], which lies on drained low-lying marshland.

